the_knock.text:
The knock comes at the hour when even the fire thinks of going out.{pause}

Three knocks. Not hurried. Not cold-handed. Three, laid on the wood like coins on a table, and then the voice — a low voice, an even voice, a voice like a road you have walked before and cannot place.

“By the old law,” it says. “A roof for the night. A place at your fire. Food, if you have it, and I know that you have little. I ask. I do not demand. The asking is the law, and the answering is yours.”

Through the gap in the shutter you see him: tall under a grey hood, leaning on a stick that is not a stick for leaning. Snow stands on his shoulders as though it has been standing there a long time. He does not stamp his feet. He does not shiver.

{kept_rites?The salt line at your door is fresh, laid an hour ago by your own hand. He is standing exactly at its edge, the way water stands at a dam.|The salt line at your door is old and broken, gone to grey slush weeks back. He is standing where the gap is.}

He waits the way stones wait.
